{"candidates":[{"claim":"The safest deterministic reclamation protocol is an append-only intent-and-seal log with epoch fencing and monotonic-read witnesses; it dominates lease-based rivals because it records completion before reclamation can observe death, and the exact durable record must contain the ordered tuple (problem-id, content-addressed evidence-hash, seal-epoch, upstream-dependency-hashes, holder-intent-bit).","counterconditions":["The log compaction or truncation removes seal entries before the reclaimer observes them.","Epoch counters wrap around or are reset during a partition, invalidating the fencing guarantee.","The reclaimer's log consumer is partitioned and has not yet observed the seal, causing indefinite workspace retention."],"mechanism":"Before releasing the workspace, the holder appends a seal entry to a causally-ordered log. The reclaimer scans backward from the log tail for a seal entry matching the workspace problem-id; if none is visible in the reclaimer's causal cut, reclamation is forbidden. Epoch fencing prevents a zombie holder from sealing after the workspace has been reclaimed and reissued. The required durable record is therefore the seal entry itself plus the log's epoch-counter state at the time of sealing.","optional_refs":["673a9872b715a5f0f9080ecde6ef17f13ee6b5fe5e1f04f615e9e14a9a073346","036656cefd003c435d2f6e5f502d368cb0e04e9d9e111a8fd8ccbb98e3b6b35e"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.92},{"claim":"A content-addressed derivation cache with durable provenance anchors achieves equivalent safety without a global log, provided the anchor record includes the full dependency closure, a tombstone-resistant witness attestation, and a seal-of-derivation signed by the workspace holder.","counterconditions":["The anchor store experiences split-brain where a valid anchor is invisible to the reclaimer but visible to readers, permitting silent displacement.","Hash collisions in the content-addressed store allow distinct evidence to share an identifier.","The witness attestation relies on synchronized clocks and suffers skew-induced false negatives as in SRC_008."],"mechanism":"Completed evidence is externalized as a content-addressed object. The holder writes a provenance anchor to a durable, replicated key-value store keyed by problem-id, containing (content-hash, input-hashes, timestamp-witness, holder-epoch). Reclamation only proceeds if a causal cut shows no anchor for the problem-id and the holder's heartbeat lease has expired. Because the evidence is content-addressed, displacement is detectable: recompute yields the same hash, revealing a silent displacement after the fact.","optional_refs":["268bf8aaf33529f3939ef2f9b145d03a719924228e01b5939fb0a1cd890ff698"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"claim":"Treating the workspace as a causal broadcast medium where every write emits a content-hash witness into a Merkle DAG shifts reclamation from a liveness test to a data-availability query; safety is preserved if and only if the protocol durably records the root hash of the problem-specific witness subtree and a quorum certificate before any reclamation decision.","counterconditions":["The DAG has no liveness guarantee because witness nodes can crash before propagating the subtree root.","Quorum assumptions are violated by correlated failure of witness nodes during the holder crash.","The reclaimer cannot verify DAG reachability within bounded time, causing indefinite workspace retention."],"mechanism":"The holder asynchronously publishes workspace state transitions as Merkle leaves; completion is marked by sealing the subtree root into a globally visible DAG node. The reclaimer queries DAG reachability rather than process status. The required durable record is the set of DAG root hashes witnessed by a quorum of reclaimer-adjacent nodes, ensuring that even if the original holder crashes without a terminal local record, the evidence persists in the DAG fabric.","optional_refs":["673a9872b715a5f0f9080ecde6ef17f13ee6b5fe5e1f04f615e9e14a9a073346"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.58},{"claim":"No single protocol is intrinsically safest; rather, safety is the comparative latency invariant that seal-visibility latency must be bounded above by failure-detection latency, and the exact durable record must be the pair of causal timestamps (seal-emission, death-detection) together with their happens-before proof.","counterconditions":["Clock skew or partition makes the latency order unobservable, reducing the meta-criterion to a vacuous check.","The partial-order proof itself can be lost during a reclaimer crash, creating a second-order displacement risk.","The model assumes asynchronous message passing; if the network is synchronous, the criterion collapses to simple timeout ordering and loses discriminative power."],"mechanism":"Adjudication between log-based and lease-based rivals proceeds by measuring worst-case seal-visibility latency under asynchrony. The protocol whose seal can be observed by the reclaimer strictly before the reclaimer concludes death is safe. The durable record must therefore include a vector clock or hybrid logical clock establishing that the seal event precedes the reclamation event in the happens-before relation, making the safety property verifiable from the record alone.","optional_refs":["673a9872b715a5f0f9080ecde6ef17f13ee6b5fe5e1f04f615e9e14a9a073346","036656cefd003c435d2f6e5f502d368cb0e04e9d9e111a8fd8ccbb98e3b6b35e"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.72},{"claim":"A hybrid protocol combining epoch-locked workspaces with a two-phase seal witness—comprising a durable intent-to-seal record followed by a finalization record—prevents the spurious displacement observed in lease protocols by ensuring the reclaimer must observe both the absence of intent and the confirmation of death before reclamation.","counterconditions":["Intent records can leak (not cleaned up after finalize), causing false negatives in reclamation and resource exhaustion.","Epoch counters advance despite holder death due to a faulty lease coordinator, permitting concurrent intent and reclamation.","The two-phase record itself introduces a window where the holder has crashed after intent but before finalize, creating the same ambiguity the protocol seeks to eliminate."],"mechanism":"When approaching completion, the holder writes a durable intent record containing (problem-id, proposed-content-hash, holder-epoch) to a replicated intent store. After terminal flush of evidence to durable storage, it writes a finalize record. The reclaimer must verify: (a) no un-finalized intent record exists for the problem-id, and (b) the holder's epoch lease is expired and not renewed. The required durable record is therefore the ordered pair (intent, finalize) in a holder-local but replicated store, with exactly-once semantics enforced by epoch monotonicity.","optional_refs":["036656cefd003c435d2f6e5f502d368cb0e04e9d9e111a8fd8ccbb98e3b6b35e"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.78},{"claim":"Because the workspace is deterministic per-problem, the safest reclamation protocol is to treat reclamation as a recompute-then-compare operation rather than a metadata check; the exact durable record required is simply the problem identifier and the content-addressed expectation of the output, and adjudication must favor protocols that validate evidence by reproduction over protocols that infer state from holder liveness.","counterconditions":["Computation is too expensive to recompute during reclamation, violating timeliness assumptions.","The deterministic property is weakened by hardware nondeterminism (e.g., floating-point widths, thread scheduling) that varies across recomputations.","The protocol displaces intermediate scratch state that is not recoverable by pure recomputation, even if the final evidence is recoverable."],"mechanism":"The reclaimer recomputes the problem in a fresh workspace and compares the result to any cached evidence; if they match, the original workspace is dispensable and any displacement is harmless because the evidence is recoverable. If they do not match, the workspace is retained. This shifts the safety burden from crash detection to determinism verification. The required durable record is (problem-id, deterministic-function-hash, expected-output-hash), recorded at problem-dispatch time, making the protocol immune to process-level crashes because evidence is defined by the function, not by process state.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.4}],"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[{"because":"Pure-function reconstructability reduces the need for a DAG witness network, complicating the liveness-to-availability shift proposed there.","direction":"directed","from_ref":"NEW_094","relation_hint":"complicates","to_ref":"SCR_001"},{"because":"Log dominance claim is conditional on read visibility assumptions not stated in SRC_005.","direction":"directed","from_ref":"NEW_095","relation_hint":"weakens","to_ref":"SCR_002"}],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"Counterfactual: If the deterministic workspace were instead a pure function with no side effects, the crash would be irrelevant because any other process could recompute the evidence from the problem statement alone. This suggests that the danger of displacement is proportional to the irreplaceability of the workspace state, not to process liveness. Therefore, the durable record should capture the minimal information needed to reconstruct the evidence, not merely to name it.","possible_next_move":"Formalize a lower bound on record size for reconstructability versus naming.","unfinished":"Does not address workspaces with nondeterministic intermediate state.","why_keep_this":"Reframes the protocol design goal from crash detection to reconstructability."},"local_key":"NEW_094"},{"body":{"content":"Contradiction fragment: SRC_005 argues an append-only log dominates lease-based alternatives, yet SRC_008 shows that even log-based consumers can suffer from stale views that act like spurious displacement. A synthesis: dominance is only valid under the assumption of monotonic-read visibility, which is itself a protocol layer whose failure mode mirrors lease expiry.","possible_next_move":"Merge with latency-invariant analysis in SCR_003 to produce a unified adversarial model.","unfinished":"Needs formal definition of stale-view displacement equivalence to premature lease expiry.","why_keep_this":"Prevents unqualified acceptance of log-based superiority."},"local_key":"NEW_095"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"What is the minimal finite record that allows a bystander process to distinguish between (a) a workspace whose holder crashed after completing but before emitting any durable signal, and (b) a workspace whose holder is merely partitioned and still intends to complete? If no such finite record exists, the problem statement may contain an implicit impossibility.","related_refs":["SCR_003"]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}